WEBVTT

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.780
After the result of the
examination had been published,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.550
Anne is filled with a sense of freedom, and enjoys
her summer vacation to her heart's content.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.980
Then, one day...

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.310
Oh, welcome back, Anne.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.250
Oh, Mrs. Lynde. How do you do?

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.310
How do you do, Miss Cuthbert, Mrs. Lynde?

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.100
Anne, we were just talking about you.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.090
Do you know about
the big hospital in Charlottetown?

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:04.400
Yes.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.330
To aid the hospital,

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.060
the hotel in White Sands plans to
organize a concert for their customers.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.870
Since they don't have
enough performances,

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.720
they have asked the neighboring
villages for support.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.830
Avonlea has decided to send
you to do a recitation.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.520
I'm to recite?

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.490
Anne, isn't that great?
Of course you will accept, won't you?

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.110
Well...

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.850
What's the matter, Anne?
It's a great honour.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:31.370
I know...

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:38.560
I don't think it's proper for young
girls to go to the hotel without an attendant,

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.790
but if you're doing this to support
the hospital in Charlottetown,

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.890
I've no reason to object.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.860
Well, I have recited poetry
several times before.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:49.880
Yes.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.480
Even though she appears to be calm,

00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:00.950
Marilla feels a great joy and pride
that Anne is receiving such an honor.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.620
After Mrs. Lynde has left, she reports
the matter to Matthew immediately.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:11.980
Of course Matthew is delighted.
He sets off to Carmody immediately,

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:15.330
and, on the recommendation of the
salesgirl he's now become familiar with,

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.310
he buys a pearl necklace at a good price.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.650
On the day of the concert,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.180
Diana helps her dress and
decide on her hairstyle.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.550
Diana is getting a reputation
for being good at these things.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.870
Do you really think the organdy will be best?

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:47.160
I don't think it's as pretty as my blue-flowered
muslin, and it certainly isn't so fashionable.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.830
But it suits you ever so much better.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.120
It's so soft and frilly and it
emphasizes your figure, right?

00:03:56.410 --> 00:04:00.210
The muslin is stiff, and makes
you look too dressed up.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.710
But the organdy seems as if it grew on you.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.180
Pull out that frill a little more.
Here, let me tie your sash.

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.190
Now for your slippers.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.560
I'm going to braid your hair in two thick braids,
and tie them halfway up with big white bows.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:30.870
No, don't pull out a single curl over your
forehead, just have the soft part.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.000
This hairstyle suits you best.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:38.980
Mrs. Allan says you look like a
Madonna when you part it so.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.760
I shall fasten this rose just behind your ear.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.510
There was just one on the bush in the
garden, and I saved it for you.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.330
Can I put on the pearl necklace?

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.930
Pearl necklace?

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.410
Matthew bought it for me,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.940
and I know he'd like to see it on me.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:05.270
Diana, what do you think?

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:09.550
Yes, you can wear it.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:12.960
Does this look good?

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:16.970
Yes. It's very nice.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.910
There's something so stylish about you, Anne.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.500
You hold your head with such an air.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.970
I suppose it's your figure.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.940
I am just a dumpling.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:35.410
I've always been afraid of it,
and now I know it is so.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.000
Well, I suppose I shall just
have to resign myself to it.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.580
Oh, but you have such nice dimples.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.420
They're so cute.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.950
They're like little dents in cream.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.730
I have given up all hope of dimples.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.530
My dimple-dream will never come true,

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.900
but so many of my dreams have
that I mustn't complain.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.010
Anne, may I come in?

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:08.540
Come right in and look at our
elocutionist. She's very beautiful.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.420
She looks neat and proper.
I like that way of fixing her hair.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.730
Diana did it for me.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.530
But I expect she'll ruin that dress driving
over there in the dust and dew with it,

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:33.330
and it looks most too
thin for these damp nights.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.770
It'll be fine, Marilla.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.370
And this is what looks best on Anne.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:39.210
Really?

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.450
Billy and Jane have come to fetch us.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.240
Come! Let's go, Anne!

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:57.990
What do you think, Matthew?

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:05.130
It's beautiful, Anne.
The audience will shout for an encore.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:06.600
An encore?

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.720
Be careful not to let your dress
touch the carriage, Anne.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:13.140
I'm leaving!

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:01.210
It's beautiful! As expected, this is
different from anything in Avonlea.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.880
Everyone looks so beautiful!

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:07.450
That's true. And our Anne is going to stand
at the best spot and recite the poem.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:08.960
Isn't it wonderful, Anne?

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:11.210
I suppose so.

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:14.440
Look! That's a diamond necklace.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.110
Amazing! That's a silk dress.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:41.610
It's still a little early.
Let's take a seat over there.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.460
Huh? Where's your brother?

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:49.600
I'm sure he's waiting outside.
He's very uncomfortable in places like this.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:03.950
It's Josie. She asked Gilbert to bring her here.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:09.610
Thank you for coming here.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:14.400
Excuse me. Everyone who is going to
perform, please go to the waiting room.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:17.360
Look! It's the lady in the pink
dress that we saw earlier.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.330
She seems to be a performer, too.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:21.100
Anne, do your best.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:22.870
Yes, I will.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.600
What's with them?
They seem very arrogant!

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:36.610
I don't know what they will do, but they
cannot possibly perform better than Anne.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:37.440
Well, hurry up, Anne!

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.490
Seeing the other participants who
are so beautifully dressed up,

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.160
Anne feels like a country bumpkin.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:53.660
The dress that had looked so elegant
in her room at Green Gables,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:57.140
now seems simple and plain.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:02.840
There seems to be many country bumpkins here today.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:07.010
They must have come to support
the local talent in the program.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:10.610
I wonder what wonderful
talents they will show us.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.290
It'll be a sight to see, don't you think?

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.380
What are you performing?

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:17.020
I'm reciting.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:20.290
Oh my! Reciting.
She said she's reciting.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:33.110
Diana, I heard just now that

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.970
they've invited a professional elocutionist
here today for a special performance.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:38.690
A professional elocutionist?

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.810
Mrs. Evans is quite famous,

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:46.850
and by chance she was staying at this hotel.
It seems they asked her to perform.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:51.850
Anne has only recited a few times in Avonlea.

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:53.860
She has bad luck tonight, don't you think?

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.890
It'll be all right.
Anne will definitely do well.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:08.950
Everyone,

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:12.680
today's concert is specially held in
support of Charlottetown's hospital.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:16.410
We're sincerely thankful that so
many have gathered here tonight.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.440
And so, let's begin immediately!

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:53.720
During the concert, the experts who came over from Charlottetown

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:56.770
are joined by people from
Carmody and Newbridge

00:11:56.900 --> 00:12:00.290
who appear one after the other
and perform outstandingly.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.890
And now, let me introduce
tonight's special guest.

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:15.370
The elocutionist Mrs. Evans.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:53.010
This morning I wished to bring you a gift of roses,

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:58.150
But I put so many in my belt which closes,

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:02.920
That the tightened knots could not contain them there,

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:09.260
And burst asunder. Winglike, the roses flew,

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:15.950
Mrs. Evans has a surprisingly flexible voice
and wonderful power of expression.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:17.710
The audience is engrossed,

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:22.000
and Anne, forgetting all about herself
and her troubles for the time,

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.610
listens with rapt and shining eyes.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:29.610
Unseen by others here
I stitch each silken flower

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:34.350
Within inward tear on tear
Yet passionless: my tower

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:38.290
Gives me the cloudless sky.
From here I see the blue,

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:43.770
Star on star espy.
I see the tempest too.

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:49.080
Opposite my own
A chair stands through the hours.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:54.360
His it was, that one;
One instant it was ours.

00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:59.700
There it stands, the chair,
A ribbon signing it,

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:06.120
As in a calm despair-
My case, placed opposite.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:17.860
I-It's impossible.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:22.860
There's no way I can go up there
and recite after that professional.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:24.090
It's impossible.

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:31.350
At this unpropitious moment,
her name is called.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:34.910
Our next performer is Miss Anne Shirley from Avonlea.

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.720
It's finally Anne's turn.

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:43.160
Good luck!

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:48.560
Anne Shirley?

00:14:48.890 --> 00:14:52.410
You're the Anne Shirley who got into
Queen's in first position?

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:07.310
What's the matter, Anne?

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:08.410
Come on.

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:45.110
Anne...

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:46.640
She's so pale.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:48.620
Jane.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:49.810
Diana.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:01.340
Oh!

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:04.770
What should I do, Diana?

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:26.360
Oh my dear father, restoration hang
Thy medicine on my lips,

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:34.390
and let this kiss repair those violent harms
that my two sisters have in thy reverence made!

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:38.240
Had you not been their father,

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:42.770
these white flakes had challeng'd pity of them.

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:48.140
Was this a face to be oppos'd against the warring winds?

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:51.880
To stand against the deep dread-bolted thunder?

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:56.630
In the most terrible and nimble stroke of quick cross lightning?

00:16:56.930 --> 00:17:00.050
Like a desperate sentry, who does not sleep.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.600
And with only his white hair as a helmet...

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:09.750
Anne's clear, sweet voice reaches to the farthest
corners of the room without a tremor or a break.

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:14.580
Self-possession is fully restored to her,
and in reaction to that horrible moment,

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:16.570
she recites as she has never done before.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.740
Then, please take care, my sisters.

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:02.350
My dear, you did splendidly!
It was really wonderful!

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:07.680
There, they're encoring you.
They're bound to have you back!

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:18.000
An encore... I can't...

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:34.160
But yet, I must do it,

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.350
because Matthew said they would encore me.

00:18:37.390 --> 00:18:40.090
If I don't go, Matthew will be disappointed.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:44.120
Yes, you mustn't disappoint Matthew!

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.750
Have you got a brook in your little heart?

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:05.170
Where bashful flowers blow,
And blushing birds go down to drink,

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:06.980
And shadows tremble so?

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.420
For Matthew's sake, Anne accedes to the encore.

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:12.390
It's a very short thing,

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:16.630
but it is cute and lovely and
captivates her audience still further.

00:19:18.370 --> 00:19:22.130
The rest of the evening is
quite a little triumph for her.

00:19:22.810 --> 00:19:24.360
After the concert,

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:28.600
the stout, pink lady takes her under her wing,
and introduces her to everybody.

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:32.580
Mrs. Evans also congratulates
Anne on her success.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:38.540
Even the white-lace girl pays
her a languid little compliment.

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:42.420
Everybody is very nice to her.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:45.520
That was great.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:48.210
I think you did better than Mrs. Evans.

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:49.300
Surely not!

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.420
It's true. That's just like you, Anne.

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:53.630
But what happened in the beginning?

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:57.640
Yes, your face was pale
and you were trembling, too.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:01.740
When you started reciting,
I was so relieved.

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:04.940
Although I thought at first you
were never going to begin.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:06.680
It's all because of Gilbert.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.060
Gilbert?

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:13.820
When I stepped on the stage,
he gave me a scornful look.

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:15.190
Did he?

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:19.420
If I gave up just like that,
he would despise me even more.

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:22.060
When I thought of that,
I was filled with courage.

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:24.090
I told myself that I must not fail.

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:27.100
Gilbert didn't look at you that way at all.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:30.730
That's right. After your performance,
he gave you a thunderous applause.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:55.090
Hasn't it been a splendid night?

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:56.210
Yes.

00:20:56.730 --> 00:21:00.130
Anne, I have something to tell
you that will make you glad.

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:03.900
At least I think it must have been a
compliment because of the tone he said it in.

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:06.030
Part of it was anyhow.

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:12.070
There was an American sitting behind Jane and me.

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:15.550
Josie Pye says he is a distinguished artist.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:19.560
Well, we heard him say, didn't we, Jane?

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:25.690
Who is that girl on the platform
with the splendid Titian hair?

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:27.920
She has a face I should like to paint.

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:29.580
What do you think, Anne?

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:33.460
But what does Titian hair mean?

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:36.990
In other words, it means plain red, I guess.

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:40.980
Titian was a very famous artist who
liked to paint red-haired women.

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:43.070
Oh, is that so?

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:47.910
Did you see all the jewels those ladies wore?

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:50.480
They were simply dazzling.

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:55.590
Everyone, wouldn't you just love to be rich?

00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:57.750
We are rich.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:01.590
Why, we have sixteen years to our credit,

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:03.560
and we're happy as queens,

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:06.900
and we've all got imaginations, more or less.

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:12.220
Everyone, look at that sea.

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:16.980
It's all silver and shallow everywhere,

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:20.460
like a vision we cannot see, isn't it?

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:30.020
Even if we had a million dollars or
countless diamond necklaces,

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:33.420
we couldn't enjoy its loveliness any more.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.460
Even if we could exchange places,

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:39.050
you wouldn't want to change with
any of those people, would you?

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.370
Would you want to be that white-lace girl,

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:46.330
who looked as if she'd been
born despising the world,

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:48.330
and wear a sour look all your life?

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:53.080
And even though I think the woman
in the pink dress must be a good person,

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:55.280
I wouldn't want to become that fat.

00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:01.520
Or even Mrs. Evans, with
that sad, sad look in her eyes?

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:05.690
She must have been dreadfully unhappy
sometime to have such a look.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:09.800
You know you wouldn't, Jane.

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:13.560
I'm not sure, exactly,

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:17.370
but diamonds are a great consolation, aren't they?

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:31.510
Anyway, I don't want to be anyone but myself,

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:34.790
even if I go uncomforted by diamonds.

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:40.210
I'm quite content to be Anne of
Green Gables, with my pearl necklace.

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:43.260
I know Matthew gave me
as much love with them

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:47.290
as ever went with the Pink Lady's jewels.

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:24.860
Anne leaves Green Gables with fond
memories to begin a new chapter in her life.

00:24:24.910 --> 00:24:27.990
Next on Anne of Green Gables:
Episode 41

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:30.170
Off to Queen's Academy

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:31.510
Please look forward to it!
